@page "/disclosure"

<PageTitle>Progressive disclosure</PageTitle>
<h1>Progressive disclosure</h1>
<p>Two different disclosure mechanisms live on this page. The traveler details section below is collapsed by pure UI state while its rule is unconditional. Submit before expanding it and the error is suppressed until the field is actually rendered; it is logged to the diagnostic below rather than shown inline. The accommodation cascade further down instead pairs each rule's <code>When</code> with the same condition that reveals its field, so its rules never produce a suppressed issue in the first place; that is the pattern for ordinary data-dependent sections.</p>

<TeachingPanel CodeFiles="@(new[] { "TravelRequest.cs", "Disclosure.razor", "Disclosure.razor.cs" })">
    <Rules>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Destination</strong>: required. A committed change to the field discloses it, no submit needed.</li>
            <li><strong>Traveler name</strong>: required. A committed change to the field discloses it, no submit needed.</li>
            <li><strong>The accommodation question</strong>: required. Answering it is the only way to engage it, and the answer is also what satisfies it, so this one speaks at a submit and nowhere else.</li>
            <li><strong>Accommodation type</strong>: required only when you answered Yes.</li>
            <li><strong>Special requirements</strong>: required only for Accessible. Both this rule and the one above mirror what the UI reveals in their <code>When()</code> conditions.</li>
            <li><strong>Required marks</strong>: derived from the same validator by <code>FormidableRequiredIndicator</code>. Destination and Traveler name carry theirs inside the label; the accommodation question's sits in the group's <code>&lt;legend&gt;</code>, outside every <code>&lt;label&gt;</code> and outside the <code>FormidableField</code> that wraps the radios, because those two labels name the options rather than the field. Placement is markup position and nothing else — the component asks the form context and its own <code>For</code>, and reads nothing from what surrounds it. Accommodation type and Special requirements wear no mark: their presence rules are conditional, and whether a condition applies cannot be answered without a model, so the component says nothing rather than asserting a demand it cannot verify. Every glyph is <code>aria-hidden</code>, so no accessible name computed from a label or a legend picks up a star; the demand itself is carried by <code>aria-required</code> on the inputs, the two radios included — wired by hand here, the way the kit's own inputs wire it for themselves.</li>
        </ul>
    </Rules>
    <TryIt>
        <ol>
            <li>Click <em>Show traveler details</em>, type into Traveler name, leave the field, clear it, leave again — the required message appears live because you acted on the field.</li>
            <li>Hide traveler details and submit — that error is suppressed into the diagnostic list below instead of appearing inline. A collapsed section cannot show a message.</li>
            <li>Show the details and submit again — the error is now inline and in the summary.</li>
            <li>Answer Yes, pick Accessible — watch the conditional cascade reveal rules only as their fields appear.</li>
            <li>Look at Special requirements before you type anything into it — it is required, it is empty, and it carries no message at all. Nothing has engaged it: the live channel answers for the fields you have committed a change to, and revealing a field is not changing it. It wears no required mark either, and that is a different silence: the mark is read from the rules alone, and this rule demands a value only under a condition nothing can evaluate without a model.</li>
            <li>Submit — the submit channel makes no such distinction, so "Describe the special requirements" lands under the box and the summary gains an entry for it.</li>
            <li>Complete everything and submit — the status line confirms.</li>
            <li>Scroll to the second form under <em>Without a summary</em> — no summary sits above
                it, and its form-level message list is empty. Click <em>Submit request</em> with
                the trip details still collapsed: every failing field is hidden, so the gate's
                "not currently displayed" explanation appears through the
                <code>FormidableModelMessage</code> directly under the heading.</li>
            <li>Click <em>Show trip details</em> and submit again — the three inline messages take
                over and the form-level explanation empties. Fill Destination and Traveler name,
                answer No, and submit: the variant's own status line confirms.</li>
        </ol>
        <p><small>Use the Submit button rather than pressing Enter — the browser's implicit submission bypasses this page's per-submit list clearing.</small></p>
    </TryIt>
</TeachingPanel>

<FormidableForm Model="_request" Options="_options" OnValidSubmit="HandleValid"
                @ref="_form">
    <div class="summary-slot">
        <FormidableSummary />
    </div>

    <div class="field"><label>Destination <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _request.Destination" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_request.Destination" /></label>
        <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _request.Destination" /></div>

    <p>
        <button type="button"
                @onclick="() => _showDetails = !_showDetails">
            @(_showDetails ? "Hide" : "Show") traveler details
        </button>
    </p>
    @if (_showDetails)
    {
        <div class="field">
            <label>Traveler name <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _request.TravelerName" />
                <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_request.TravelerName" /></label>
            <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _request.TravelerName" />
        </div>
    }

    <fieldset>
        <legend>Do you need accommodation? <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _request.NeedsAccommodation" /></legend>
        <FormidableField For="() => _request.NeedsAccommodation" Context="field">
            <label><input type="radio" name="needs" id="@field.ElementId"
                          aria-invalid="@(field.AriaInvalid ? "true" : null)"
                          aria-describedby="@field.AriaDescribedBy"
                          aria-required="@(field.Requirement == FieldRequirement.Required ? "true" : null)"
                          checked="@(_request.NeedsAccommodation == true)"
                          @onchange="() => SetNeeds(true, field)" /> Yes</label>
            <label><input type="radio" name="needs"
                          aria-required="@(field.Requirement == FieldRequirement.Required ? "true" : null)"
                          checked="@(_request.NeedsAccommodation == false)"
                          @onchange="() => SetNeeds(false, field)" /> No</label>
        </FormidableField>
        <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _request.NeedsAccommodation" />
    </fieldset>

    @if (_request.NeedsAccommodation == true)
    {
        <fieldset>
            <legend>Accommodation</legend>
            <FormidableField For="() => _request.AccommodationType" Context="field">
                <label>Type
                    <select id="@field.ElementId" class="@field.CssClass"
                            aria-invalid="@(field.AriaInvalid ? "true" : null)"
                            aria-describedby="@field.AriaDescribedBy"
                            value="@_request.AccommodationType"
                            @onchange="args => OnTypeChanged(args, field)">
                        <option value="">Choose…</option>
                        <option>Hotel</option>
                        <option>Serviced apartment</option>
                        <option>Accessible</option>
                    </select>
                </label>
            </FormidableField>
            <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _request.AccommodationType" />

            @if (_request.AccommodationType == "Accessible")
            {
                <div class="field">
                    <label>Special requirements
                        <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_request.SpecialRequirements" /></label>
                    <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _request.SpecialRequirements" />
                </div>
            }
        </fieldset>
    }

    <div class="actions"><button type="button" class="primary" @onclick="Submit">Submit</button></div>
</FormidableForm>

<p role="status">@_status</p>

<h2>Suppressed-issue diagnostic</h2>
<p><small>Shows the issues the form above suppressed at its most recent submit — a fully disclosed submit leaves it empty.</small></p>
<ul>
    @foreach (var message in _suppressed)
    {
        <li>@message</li>
    }
</ul>

<section id="inline-only-variant">
    <h2>Without a summary</h2>
    <p>The defensive gate's explanation is a model-level issue, and the summary is only one of the
        places it lands. This form renders no <code>FormidableSummary</code> at all: a
        <code>FormidableModelMessage</code> sits above the fields instead — a persistent list,
        empty until the form has something to say about itself. Submit while the trip details are
        collapsed and the gate's explanation appears through it; reveal the section and submit
        again, and the inline messages take over while the form-level list empties.</p>

    <FormidableForm Model="_inlineRequest" Options="_inlineOptions"
                    OnValidSubmit="HandleInlineValid">
        <FormidableModelMessage />

        <p>
            <button type="button" @onclick="() => _showInlineSection = !_showInlineSection">
                @(_showInlineSection ? "Hide" : "Show") trip details
            </button>
        </p>
        @if (_showInlineSection)
        {
            <div class="field"><label>Destination <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _inlineRequest.Destination" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_inlineRequest.Destination" /></label>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _inlineRequest.Destination" /></div>

            <div class="field"><label>Traveler name <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _inlineRequest.TravelerName" /> <FormidableInputText @bind-Value="_inlineRequest.TravelerName" /></label>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _inlineRequest.TravelerName" /></div>

            <fieldset>
                <legend>Do you need accommodation? <FormidableRequiredIndicator For="() => _inlineRequest.NeedsAccommodation" /></legend>
                <FormidableField For="() => _inlineRequest.NeedsAccommodation" Context="field">
                    <label><input type="radio" name="inline-needs" id="@field.ElementId"
                                  aria-invalid="@(field.AriaInvalid ? "true" : null)"
                                  aria-describedby="@field.AriaDescribedBy"
                                  aria-required="@(field.Requirement == FieldRequirement.Required ? "true" : null)"
                                  checked="@(_inlineRequest.NeedsAccommodation == true)"
                                  @onchange="() => SetInlineNeeds(true, field)" /> Yes</label>
                    <label><input type="radio" name="inline-needs"
                                  aria-required="@(field.Requirement == FieldRequirement.Required ? "true" : null)"
                                  checked="@(_inlineRequest.NeedsAccommodation == false)"
                                  @onchange="() => SetInlineNeeds(false, field)" /> No</label>
                </FormidableField>
                <FormidableFieldMessage For="() => _inlineRequest.NeedsAccommodation" />
            </fieldset>
        }

        <div class="actions"><button type="submit" class="primary">Submit request</button></div>
    </FormidableForm>

    <p role="status">@_inlineStatus</p>
</section>
